# Written by PanelAlpha.
#
# cb_install/ is the web installer, and it is inside the document root
# (upload/). Upstream gates it on one file -- upload/files/temp/install.me,
# which hooks/prepare.sh deletes and which this deployment never creates -- and
# that gate is enough on its own: with the file absent, cb_install/index.php
# redirects to the site root (cb_install/functions_install.php:2-11) and
# cb_install/ajax.php returns without doing anything (ajax.php:11).
#
# This file is the second lock, because of what is behind the first one:
#
#   ajax.php `create_files` writes upload/includes/config.php -- a PHP file the
#   application includes on every request -- by substituting $_POST['dbhost'],
#   ['dbname'], ['dbuser'], ['dbpass'], ['dbport'] and ['dbprefix'] into
#   cb_install/config.php's single-quoted PHP strings with no escaping of any
#   kind (ajax.php:183-195). A password containing `' . <expression> . '` is
#   arbitrary PHP in that file. Unauthenticated, from the network.
#
#   ajax.php also opens a mysqli connection to whatever host and port it is
#   posted and reports the outcome (ajax.php:29-56), which is a connect probe
#   for anything the container can reach.
#
#   index.php's `adminsettings` and `finish` modes set the administrator's
#   username, password and email from POST (modes/finish.php:2-10).
#
# One deleted file between a public URL and that is not a margin worth keeping,
# and nothing in a deployed ClipBucket ever fetches a URL under cb_install/:
# the directory's other use is the SQL that AdminTool and Update read off the
# filesystem. Denying it also stops cb_install/sql/*.sql being served as plain
# text, which Apache would otherwise happily do with the schema and the whole
# of the English translation table.
#
# An account that genuinely wants upstream's wizard back has to delete this
# file *and* create upload/files/temp/install.me, which is a deliberate act
# rather than an accident.
Require all denied
